Getting started, one guide per target

GETTING_STARTED.md described setup for the only target that exists, as though it were
setup for the framework. Renamed to GETTING_STARTED_TARGET_SCALA.md, with stubs beside it
for Haskell and TypeScript, and one structure across all three.

WHAT THE STRUCTURE ASSERTS. Nine sections, identical in every file — verified, the only
heading that differs is the target name in section 4. Section 0 states the loop (author a
definition, emit its surface, project it, register it, verify) and says plainly that
nothing in it belongs to a target. Only four things do: the toolchain, the projection
command, how you run the result, and the command set. That claim is the point of the
split; the parallel files are what make it checkable rather than asserted.

README's own getting-started section became a duplicate under that split, so it now
carries the neutral loop, a pointer to the three guides, and the example definition — which
is genuinely target-independent text — while the commands live in the per-target guide.

THE STUBS ARE NOT PLACEHOLDERS. Each carries the open questions its target must answer,
because those are what experience will fill in and they are worth writing down while the
reasons are fresh. Haskell's hardest is derivation: Scala's extends/with is subtyping and
Haskell has none, so a derivation becomes a constraint, an embedded record, or a sum — and
that choice determines what `globals` and `typeDefinition` mean there. TypeScript's is
identity: draco's TypeName identity is nominal, TypeScript's type system is structural, so
`Meters` and `Radians` would be interchangeable by default. Whatever that target does about
branding IS its semantic-preservation story, and it should be decided rather than fallen
into.

Each file also names what its target tests about the framework. Haskell stresses whether
the STRUCTURE is neutral, being the target least like Scala. TypeScript stresses whether
IDENTITY is. Both are expected to find residues, and those belong in README's
Language-specific residues table when they do.

Also recorded on the favourable side: the surface language already uses Haskell-form
spellings — one `->` for both function types and function values, `\p -> body`, if/then/
else — chosen for neutrality, and they should project directly.

TWO CORRECTIONS FOUND WHILE WRITING. `bin/draco-gen` has no `from-yaml`/`to-yaml` (removed
with YAML) and no `drake` subcommand; the surface CLI is a separate `draco.DrakeCLI` with
`emit | parse | check` and no bin wrapper. The old guide listed the YAML pair, and README —
written two days ago — invoked a `draco-gen drake` that does not exist. Both fixed, and the
Scala guide now carries a verified command table for all three CLIs.

